import{_ as i,P as n,d as a,e as r,y as o,q as s,f as d}from"./chunks/framework.D7CAgLCq.js";const b=JSON.parse(`{"title":"A.1 HV01 Structural Domain (Full Interface Card)","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"A.1 HV01 Structural Domain (Full Interface Card)","xkChars":0,"xkWords":1221,"xkLessons":{"A.1":{"title":"A.1 HV01 Structural domain (complete interface card)","lead":"HV01 first registers candidate human objects, then tests bounded grouping gain through G1. A shared name does not imply a unified will, still less authority to impose dispositions.","explanation":["Source location: Chinese source lines 3654–3719; original site location /theory/appendix-a/a-01#section-a-1. See the theory-v2 source for the current full text; historical research drafts and excerpts remain available for checking in their original downloadable versions.","Short quotation translated from Chinese: “D0 only declares a candidate human object.” HV01 first permits clarification of “which people, materials, boundaries, and window we are studying,” without requiring prior proof that this grouping has become an effective structural domain. It separates candidate naming from preregistered G1-instance support, preventing names, shared locations, or common problems from being substituted for an objectively unified will. [T-main；T-D0]","R0 (HV01-R0-candidate-object), when D0 is complete but no eligible and supported G1-instance exists yet, permits registration of the candidate object, material set, boundary disputes, and needs for supplementary evidence. R0 may pass object references carrying scope and uncertainty to HV02–HV11, so incomplete G1 need not silence all subsequent description; downstream uses must not present the reference as a supported structural domain. [T-main]","R1 (HV01-R1-effective-domain) requires a preregistered G1-instance to achieve supported within the same object, scale, window, K, and generalization unit, and adds the E4 protocol. It supports bounded grouping gain exceeding a threshold on preselected outcomes relative to matched N0, with scope bound to preregistered SP/T/K and generalization_unit. Domain grounds for an object’s real existence differ from predictive/explanatory grouping gain; gain cannot prove ultimate ontology, collective will, or jurisdiction. [T-main；T-G1]","R0/R1 are not rankings of personality or organizational maturity. Existing materials may support meaningful boundary observations without supporting formal G1; conversely, grouping gain for an outcome cannot imply all members’ wishes coincide or permit dispositions against excluded people. Cross-scale use must add units and populations, representativeness, the J axis, and low-visibility positions; local members cannot speak for everyone affected. [T-main]","Appeals focus on boundaries, representativeness, and identity criteria, and must be safely available without retaliation to members and affected positions, leading to review outside the original naming/decision chain. Authorized rollback does more than change the structural domain’s name: it must cancel its effect on downstream object scope, restore the material set, preserve versions, and verify completion. Actual record and rights adjustments still require valid authority. [T-main]","DWP’s FRS 2023–2024 distinguishes household, family/benefit unit, and individual levels within the same survey. This provides a real-world comparison for registering object criteria, but official definitions alone cannot establish G1, nor can a statistical unit define all family rights. The following 39 fields retain every registration in the original card and separately explain their responsibilities. [P-FRS；T-main]","Field-by-field study of all 39 fields (“Original card” below retains the field’s complete content in translation; “Interpretation” specifies its role and irreplaceable responsibility).","01 | Interface ID (id). Original card: HV01. Interpretation: the short identifier within the cardbook tracks the same interface; it does not indicate that a real-world object has been proved. [T-main]","02 | Qualified ID (qualified_id). Original card: human_variable:HV01. Interpretation: the namespace prevents naming collisions; references to this ID must still carry object and evidential status. [T-main]","03 | Name (name). Original card: Structural domain. Interpretation: the name provides a reading entry point, not an automatic label for particular people, groups, or real structures. [T-main]","04 | Claim type (claim_type). Original card: H. Interpretation: H identifies the role of a human-instance/interface proposition; it cannot be upgraded into formal proof or an unconditional law of the world. [T-main]","05 | Contract role (contract_role). Original card: human_variable_interface. Interpretation: this variable-interface contract specifies how to enter analysis; a field’s existence does not mean an empirical instance has been established. [T-main]","06 | Proposition (proposition). Original card: D0 only declares a candidate human object; a bounded effective structural domain is registered within an instance’s scope only when a preregistered G1-instance shows that the candidate grouping achieves out-of-sample or generalization gain above the threshold on preselected outcomes relative to matched N0. Interpretation: there are two levels of eligibility: D0 suffices to declare a candidate; only proportionate generalization gain over matched N0 under preregistered G1 supports a bounded effective structural domain. These cannot be reversed. [T-main]","07 | Scope (scope). Original card: Relationships, teams, organizations, institutions, and public issues. Interpretation: scope lists task domains that may be studied, not empirical support for every object within them. [T-main]","08 | Pause condition (pause_condition). Original card: Object, boundary, scale, time window, or identity is insufficient to support the selected route; when applying for formal G1 support, the corresponding null model or instance contract is incomplete. Interpretation: pausing concerns dependent judgment or adoption; independent descriptions may remain, but failed premises cannot continue to be used. [T-main]","09 | Inferential dependencies (inferential_requires). Original card: 1. D0. Interpretation: inferential dependencies provide upstream content needed for the proposition to hold; upstream candidate/support status must pass through unchanged, without automatic upgrading by citation. [T-main]","10 | Protocol dependencies (protocol_requires). Original card: 1. E1; 2. EVIDENCE; 3. SOURCE. Interpretation: protocol dependencies constrain evidence gathering and expression; completing protocols does not independently prove an empirical proposition true. [T-main]","11 | Qualification / specialization (specializes). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: an empty set means only that no such specialization relations are registered, not that evidential responsibility is absent; a nonempty set also does not imply universal validity. [T-main]","12 | Applicable-object references (applies_to). Original card: None (empty set). Interpretation: applicable-object references cannot replace the current object contract; an empty set is explicit registration, not permission to apply to every object. [T-main]","13 | Conditional support routes (conditional_support_routes). Original card: 1. route_id=HV01-R0-candidate-object; claim_level=candidate_description; when=The D0 object contract is complete, but no eligible G1-instance with result_state=supported exists yet.; additional_inferential_requires=None (empty set); additional_protocol_requires=None (empty set); allowed_conclusion=Register the candidate human object, material set, boundary disputes, and G1 supplementary-evidence needs.; result_ceiling=Candidate-object description only; may provide HV02-HV11 with object references carrying boundaries and uncertainty, without treating those references as effective structural domains already supported by G1. 2. route_id=HV01-R1-effective-domain; claim_level=descriptive_classification; when=A preregistered G1-instance achieves supported within the same object, scale, window, K, and generalization unit.; additional_inferential_requires=G1-instance; additional_protocol_requires=E4; allowed_conclusion=Register the bounded effective structural domain within that instance’s scope, strength of grouping-gain support, robustness of that gain to tested boundary perturbations, and applicable window.; result_ceiling=Limited to preregistered SP/T/K and generalization_unit; no judgments of ultimate ontology, unified will, or authorization. Interpretation: R0 may pass candidate objects to other HVs while retaining uncertainty; R1 requires an eligible G1-instance=supported and E4. R0’s weaker conditions must not be connected to R1’s effective-structural-domain conclusion. [T-main]","14 | Allowed inference (allowed_inference). Original card: 1. R0 may register candidate objects, boundary disputes, and bounded descriptive references; 2. Register the bounded effective structural domain supported by its gain and the strength of identification only within the G1-instance’s preregistered object, scale, window, outcome, and generalization unit. Interpretation: these are permitted directions of conclusion; which is actually reached and with what strength remain limited by the selected route and materials. [T-main]","15 | Prohibited leap (prohibited_leap). Original card: 1. Naming means an objective community; 2. Shared circumstances mean shared willingness. Interpretation: these are common boundary violations, not avoided by rewording; inspect actual inference and downstream use, not wording alone. [T-main]","16 | Nine-axis scale profile (scale_profile). Original card: SP=<A,X,T,O,C,R,I,N,J>; A=aggregation level: relationship or event units, candidate member population, distributions inside/outside the boundary, and grouping rules; X=spatial scope: shared places, organizational boundaries, digital platforms, and cross-domain external environments; T=time span: identification window, membership-change cycles, and boundary history; O=organizational level: roles, teams, organizations, institutions, through governance ecosystems; C=causal level: original events, interaction mechanisms, meso-level relational structures, institutional and systemic conditions; R=observational resolution: original interactions, event sequences, individual member cases, boundary distributions, indicators, and summaries, with compression loss registered; I=sphere of influence: direct members, excluded people, indirectly affected people, second-order spillovers, cross-domain and intergenerational positions; N=network-topological scope: member relations, boundary connections, isolated nodes, and cross-domain bridges; J=jurisdiction and authorization scope: register authorization separately for object naming, boundary adoption, and subsequent disposition; inapplicable axes must have reasons for not_applicable registered. Interpretation: register all nine axes individually; particularly, sphere of influence I creates no jurisdictional authority J, and lower observational resolution R requires retention of compression loss. [T-main]","17 | Effective object (effective_object). Original card: The candidate object declared by D0; registered as a bounded effective structural domain within that instance’s scope only after passing the preregistered G1-instance threshold test relative to matched N0. Interpretation: a candidate object is not nonexistent; “bounded effective” here specifically denotes the scope supported by a G1-instance test, not a verdict on the object’s ultimate reality. [T-main]","18 | Cross-scale invariants (scale_invariants). Original card: 1. Object contract; 2. Participating and affected positions. Interpretation: invariants are comparative responsibilities retained during transfer, not a declaration that variable values remain constant at every scale. [T-main]","19 | Required additions on elevation (required_scale_additions). Original card: 1. Units and populations; 2. Representativeness; 3. J axis; 4. Low-visibility positions. Interpretation: elevation is not copying and enlarging the original table. Where materials on new populations, interfaces, responsibility positions, and so on are incomplete, restrict conclusions dependent on them. [T-main]","20 | Semantics changing with scale (changing_semantics). Original card: 1. Effective members, relations, and identity may change with scale. Interpretation: the same word may change meaning when the object or scale changes; reconstruct correspondence rather than assume the original meaning applies unchanged. [T-main]","21 | Inapplicable objects (non_applicable_objects). Original card: 1. Nonhuman systems without interfaces for intention, institutions, or responsibility. Interpretation: inapplicability constraints prevent unconditional projection of human normative, responsibility, and experiential interfaces; they do not deny that other theories can study these objects. [T-main]","22 | Forbidden elevation (forbidden_elevation). Original card: 1. A local group directly representing all affected parties. Interpretation: inference from local to whole requires separate proof; broad impact, a grand name, or formal similarity cannot supply representativeness and authorization. [T-main]","23 | State set (state). Original card: 1. Candidate; 2. Identifiable; 3. Boundary disputed; 4. Not established. Interpretation: states are descriptive classifications requiring evidence, not a maturity ladder for scoring people; state changes require windows, grounds, and versions. [T-main]","24 | Observables (observables). Original card: 1. Differences in relational density and constraints inside/outside boundaries; 2. Records of members entering, exiting, and being excluded; 3. Repeated co-occurrence of common problems, resource channels, or institutional rules; 4. Whether grouping gain remains stable after grouping rules change. Interpretation: observable indications tell us where to seek materials; a single indication’s presence is not proof of a complete mechanism or causal effect. [T-main]","25 | Evidence requirements (evidence). Original card: 1. D0 candidate-object and identity records; 2. G1-instance preregistration form and matched N0; 3. Training and out-of-sample or generalization results; 4. Gain comparisons between candidate and competing groupings. Interpretation: this column includes materials needed when applying for G1; it must not be read as requiring all G1 steps before everyday R0 description or ordinary services. Evidence gathering follows the particular route and task relevance. [T-main]","26 | Input dependencies and interface content (input_dependencies). Original card: 1. D0 provides candidate-object fields only, not evidence that the structure is established; 2. Preregistered G1-instance and matched N0, thresholds, model class, samples or generalization units; 3. Observation positions, competing groupings, and E1 protocol. Interpretation: this states what arrives from upstream and its eligibility; gaps cannot quietly disappear while passing between interfaces. [T-main]","27 | Output effects and variable flow (output_effects). Original card: 1. R0 provides the remaining variables with candidate-object references, boundaries, time windows, and uncertainty; R1 additionally provides the bounded effective scope supported by G1; 2. Grounds for object existence and domain facts remain separate from grouping gain. Interpretation: variable flow identifies downstream judgments that may depend on this interface; passing references guarantees neither downstream conclusions, actual repair, nor authorization. [T-main]","28 | Time window and lag (time_window_and_lag). Original card: Register identification windows, boundary changes, and membership-change lags. Interpretation: occurrence, observation, execution, and arrival of effects may differ in time; short-term materials cannot automatically certify extensions beyond the window. [T-main]","29 | Uncertainty (uncertainty). Original card: Record boundary disputes, absent members, and observational coverage. Interpretation: direct uncertainty toward specific materials and positions; an overall confidence score cannot conceal fundamentally uncovered positions. [T-main]","30 | Local exclusion zone (local_exclusion_zone). Original card: Positions unable to express themselves safely or not sampled must not be represented by the aggregate. Interpretation: exclusion zones limit current inferential coverage, not delete people or facts from the world; unavailability for safety is neither consent nor absence of problems. [T-main]","31 | Affected positions (affected_positions). Original card: 1. Members; 2. Excluded people; 3. Cost bearers outside the boundary. Interpretation: register directly and indirectly affected parties together; visible members do not represent every cost bearer. [T-main]","32 | Uptake carrier (carrier). Original card: 1. Relational networks; 2. Organizational boundaries; 3. Institutional records. Interpretation: a carrier is the structure or process that actually carries something; its name, completed construction, and arrival of the required function are different evidence. [T-main]","33 | Responsible party (responsible_subject). Original card: 1. Analysts proposing structural-domain judgments; 2. Decision-makers using those judgments. Interpretation: identify who proposes, operates, or uses judgments; recording responsibility is not automatically a legal-liability adjudication or new authorization. [T-main]","34 | Normative status (normative_status). Original card: A descriptive H-World interface; generates no justification. Interpretation: distinguish descriptive, normative, and bridging status according to the card. Established facts do not independently determine value rankings; normative reasons do not automatically prove empirical effects. [T-main]","35 | Judgment ceiling (judgment_ceiling). Original card: Only when G1-instance passes, and only within the preregistered object, scale, window, outcome, and generalization unit, may an explanatory-level bounded effective structural domain be registered; otherwise do not register grouping-gain support, while candidate-object references and independently grounded object facts remain as usual. Interpretation: the ceiling here is an explanatory-level bounded effective structural domain supported by grouping gain and bound to preregistered outcomes and generalization units. Without G1 support, candidate references and independent object facts still remain according to their respective grounds. [T-main]","36 | Action ceiling (action_ceiling). Original card: This variable produces only descriptions of candidate structural domains, boundary disputes, and supplementary-evidence needs; it authorizes no inclusion, exclusion, or disposition. Any real adjustment must separately pass C12, explicit runtime N premises, J authorization, and O procedures. Interpretation: the interface itself creates no real-world authority to impose dispositions; actual actions separately require checking choice, C12, explicit N, valid J, O, protections, and periods. [T-main]","37 | Counterexamples (counterexamples). Original card: 1. People repeatedly co-occurring in one location have no stable relations or common constraints; 2. A group delineated by an analyst disappears immediately when grouping rules change. Interpretation: counterexamples restrict unconditional extrapolation; they may require narrowing scope or gathering evidence anew, without automatically negating every bounded use. [T-main]","38 | Appeal (appeal). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, members and affected positions may challenge boundaries, representativeness, and identity criteria through safely accessible channels protected against retaliation, triggering review independent of the original naming or decision chain. Interpretation: appeals must be safely accessible, free from retaliation, and capable of triggering independent review; a name alone does not mean authority to correct errors exists. [T-main]","39 | Rollback (rollback). Original card: Under appeal_and_rollback_rule, when rollback is authorized, a designated responsible person must actually revoke the structural-domain registration within the specified period, remove its effect on downstream object scope, and return it to candidate grouping. Independently grounded object facts are not thereby revoked; preserve versions and completion verification. Interpretation: actually cancel erroneous effects within valid authorization, correct downstream uses, and verify completion; changing document wording does not restore rights and resources to the affected person. [T-main]","Read the complete card’s outputs and rollback together: R1 supports only grouping gain and robustness to boundary perturbations within preregistered SP/T/K and generalization units. Revoking the corresponding structural-domain registration and its downstream gain effect does not revoke object facts with other reliable grounds. R0 may still provide candidate references carrying boundaries and uncertainty; the card state “not established” cannot be expanded into denial of people’s existence."],"argumentSteps":["Use D0 to fix object, boundary, scale, window, and K.","Without an eligible supported G1 instance yet, follow R0 and output only candidate descriptions and supplementary-evidence needs.","When applying for R1, freeze outcomes, model class, matched N0, thresholds, and generalization units, then judge through the G1 instance.","Limit instance support to preregistered scope; downstream recipients receive the same evidential status.","Members, excluded people, and cost bearers outside boundaries can all challenge representation and boundaries.","If a boundary judgment is withdrawn, eliminate downstream effects and verify through valid procedures; do not impose dispositions directly from the interface."],"distinctions":[{"leftTitle":"Candidate object","leftText":"D0 has specified the study’s referent.","rightTitle":"Bounded effective structural domain","rightText":"A particular preregistered G1 instance supports its bounded gain."},{"leftTitle":"Grouping is useful","leftText":"A gain holds for the specified outcome and comparison.","rightTitle":"Unified will","rightText":"Cannot be inferred from statistical grouping."},{"leftTitle":"Grounds for an object’s existence","leftText":"Domain facts establish that people or relationships exist.","rightTitle":"Grounds for G1 gain","rightText":"Compare whether grouping improves the specified outcome; neither substitutes for the other."},{"leftTitle":"Descriptive reference","leftText":"May be passed to downstream HV interfaces while retaining candidate status.","rightTitle":"Transmission of formal support","rightText":"A reference alone cannot automatically elevate R0 to R1."}],"boundaries":["Counterexample in the original card: people repeatedly co-occurring at the same place have no stable relationships or shared constraints.","Counterexample in the original card: a delineated group disappears immediately when the grouping rules change.","Teaching counterexample: declaring that everyone agrees to the same representative because they are jointly affected by a policy skips wishes and authorization of representation.","Boundary in the original: positions unable to express themselves safely or not sampled must not be directly represented by the aggregate."],"questions":["Does absence of G1 support mean no relationship or boundary may be described?","If grouping gain is sufficient, can a shared will be declared?","After R1 is withdrawn, is deleting the name enough?","Does an empty specializes mean no evidence is required?"],"diagramPath":"","caseLinks":[{"title":"Different levels of statistical objects in the FRS","href":"https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024"}],"caseNotes":[{"title":"Different levels of statistical objects in the FRS","text":"DWP’s 2023–2024 Family Resources Survey distinguishes three levels—co-resident statistical household, family/benefit unit, and individual—and defines the first two.","sourceLabel":"DWP：Family Resources Survey 2023—2024","sourceUrl":"https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024","sourceLocator":"§2, Households, benefit units and individuals; web page lines 171–184","boundary":"Supports only the distinguishability of definitions; it does not test G1 or establish any group’s shared will, general legal family boundaries, or authority to act.","href":"https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024"}],"sourceRefs":[{"section":"A.1","start_line":3745,"end_line":3810,"startLine":3745,"endLine":3810}],"outputId":"section-a-1","sourceHash":"51795bb7988f2669a324a52666c320cfba1382f597f7b5c6a1431844572da1fb","teachingExamples":[{"title":"Teaching construction: crossing a numerical threshold is only part of an instance judgment","text":"Suppose an outcome score for which higher is better, a matched N0, and a 5-point threshold are frozen in advance; in the same extrapolation evaluation, the candidate grouping scores 78 and matched N0 scores 70, giving a difference of 8 points, above 5. This completes only the numerical comparison in the setup; conditions including valid freezing, independent evidence, matching of comparisons, error, and decision rules are still required. 8>5 alone cannot establish that a real G1 is supported. If the scope is this type of team this quarter, it also cannot be extrapolated nationwide or to shared will.","assumptions":"All scores and thresholds are teaching constructions, not measured samples or an actual G1 instance.","boundary":"An artificial threshold crossing is not used as formal supported status, still less as real-world authority to include or exclude."},{"title":"Teaching construction: candidate references can still be used","text":"Constructed materials concern residents of the same building, but G1 has not been conducted. R0 may fix the candidate resident scope and absent people, then pass it to HV02 to describe entrances, exits, and resource interfaces; “a building roster exists” must not be rewritten as everyone’s wishes, nor may ordinary safety support be refused because G1 has not been conducted.","assumptions":"The building and people are fictional; no personal data are involved.","boundary":"An available descriptive entry point does not establish its causal effects or all domain facts."}],"counterexamples":[{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the original card: people repeatedly co-occurring at the same place have no stable relationships or shared constraints.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Counterexample in the original card: a delineated group disappears immediately when the grouping rules change.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"constructed_boundary","text":"Teaching counterexample: declaring that everyone agrees to the same representative because they are jointly affected by a policy skips wishes and authorization of representation.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]},{"identity":"source_counterexample","text":"Boundary in the original: positions unable to express themselves safely or not sampled must not be directly represented by the aggregate.","effect":"Limits unconditional extension of this section without negating independently grounded bounded judgments.","sourceIds":["T-main"]}],"questionAnswers":[{"question":"Does absence of G1 support mean no relationship or boundary may be described?","answer":"No; with complete D0, the R0 candidate-description route can be used, passing references with uncertainty to other HV interfaces.","basis":"HV01-R0。"},{"question":"If grouping gain is sufficient, can a shared will be declared?","answer":"No; R1 supports only preregistered outcomes and scope, not unified will or authorization.","basis":"R1 ceiling and prohibited leaps."},{"question":"After R1 is withdrawn, is deleting the name enough?","answer":"No; downstream effects must be withdrawn through valid procedures, versions preserved, and completion verified.","basis":"Original card, rollback."},{"question":"Does an empty specializes mean no evidence is required?","answer":"No; it means only that this type of dependency is an empty set; other contracts remain effective.","basis":"Appendix introduction and section B."}],"figures":[],"diagramBrief":"This page uses the current text to explain the v2 distinction between object facts and grouping gain; the original research figure remains available among the historical-material downloads.","applicability":{"realCase":"applicable","diagram":"applicable","reason":"This unit concerns methodological judgment. Actual sources support only the listed facts; the diagram and teaching construction each retain their own status."},"openQuestions":["No real preregistered G1-instance or results are available; all numbers are teaching constructions, and no supported status is issued.","Representativeness, absent positions, safe appeals, independent review, and rollback for a specific object require actual records and authority."],"taskId":"S-A-1","sources":[{"id":"T-main","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§A.1, Chinese source lines 3568–3633","claim_support":["All 39 HV01 fields and R0/R1 conditional routes."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-intro","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§intro-17-appendix-a, Chinese source lines 3562–3567","claim_support":["Five zones, 39 fields, material statuses, empty sets, and complete-route reading."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-D0","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§3.1, Chinese source lines 425–450","claim_support":["D0 definition of candidate objects."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"T-G1","title":"Historical research manuscript: Xikari, On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation","url":"/reading-assets/theory.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"manuscript-20261013-df579dbdbb68; the text dates itself to October 2026","locator":"§4.3, Chinese source lines 602–609","claim_support":["Conditional gain from candidate grouping under G1."],"limits":["Supports the current manuscript's textual meaning and explicitly bounded inference; does not by itself establish empirical universality, implementation effects or readers' learning outcomes.","This entry preserves the original research’s version, locations, and reading scope; it is not a record of external-source review for v2. For the current theory, see theory-v2."],"access_status":"read"},{"id":"P-FRS","title":"DWP：Family Resources Survey 2023—2024","url":"https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024/family-resources-survey-financial-year-2023-to-2024","source_type":"primary","version_or_date":"2023–2024 survey; page updated 2026-01-15","locator":"§2, Households, benefit units and individuals; web page lines 171–184","claim_support":["The same survey distinguishes household, family/benefit unit, and individual statistical levels, defining the first two."],"limits":["The researcher specifically read the definitions. They establish statistical object criteria only, not G1 grouping gain, and do not make a benefit unit a general legal family."],"access_status":"partially_read"},{"id":"theory-v2","title":"Xikari: On Conditions, Structure, and Transformation, v2, November 2026","url":"/downloads/Xikari-条件结构与转化论.md","source_type":"theory_source","version_or_date":"v2 / 2026-11-16","locator":"A.1 HV01 Structural Domain (Complete Interface Card); Chinese source lines 3654–3719; SHA-256 6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c","claim_support":["The current source section and this text’s conceptual explanation of v2; not a substitute for external empirical materials."],"limits":["Older research attachments retain their original manuscript identity; the previous scope of external reading has not expanded because of this edition change."],"access_status":"read"}],"lecturePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-1/lecture.md","sourcesPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-1/sources.json","sourceSlicePath":"/reading-assets/S-A-1/source/S-A-1.md","supplementaryFiles":[],"diagramsPath":"/reading-assets/S-A-1/diagrams.md","editionNote":"The relevant explanation in this section has been revised for v2; the original research attachments and the scope of external-source review retain their historical status.","translationOf":{"language":"zh","lesson_sha256":"4079e4d4a3a38921607662fc3d07815afd3894dc2709010ae59e137a68f98b4d","source_sha256":"6ef2e508474d4ac1ad07d7e4e068955342c405b5f680afcfc40d68cca3d0149c"}}}},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-01.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-a/a-01.md"}`),c={name:"en/theory/appendix-a/a-01.md"};function l(u,e,p,h,m,g){const t=n("SectionLesson");return a(),r("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=o("",1)),s(t,{section:"A.1"}),e[1]||(e[1]=d("div",{class:"theory-source"},null,-1))])}const y=i(c,[["render",l]]);export{b as __pageData,y as default};
